    <section id="overview"><h2>Generative modelling and diffusion</h2>
<p>Generative models aim to produce new samples resembling the training distribution. Diffusion models do this by learning to reverse a gradual corruption process. During training, noise is added to clean data at different time steps, and a neural network learns to predict the noise or another equivalent target. During generation, the model starts from noise and repeatedly denoises.</p><p>The supplied Week 11 slides cover the generative landscape, evaluation, diffusion, DDIM, guidance and latent diffusion.</p>
</section>
<section id="forward"><h2>Forward noising process</h2>
<p>The forward process is a fixed corruption process that gradually adds Gaussian noise. A common parameterization samples a noisy image directly at timestep t:</p><div class="eq">\(x_t=\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}x_0+\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\,\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,I)\)</div><p>Here \(x_0\) is the clean sample, \(x_t\) is the noisy version, and \(\bar\alpha_t\) controls how much signal remains. At larger t, less original structure remains and the sample approaches noise.</p>
</section>
<section id="reverse"><h2>Learning to denoise</h2>
<p>A neural network, often a U-Net for images, receives a noisy sample and a time-step embedding and predicts the noise added to it. A common objective is mean squared error between predicted and actual noise:</p><div class="eq">\(L=\mathbb E_{x_0,t,\epsilon}[\|\epsilon-\epsilon_\theta(x_t,t)\|_2^2]\)</div><p>At inference, begin with random noise and apply the learned reverse update repeatedly. The same trained denoiser is reused at multiple time steps. The network does not simply output the final clean image in one pass.</p><p>The connection to earlier weeks is useful: MSE is a familiar loss, and U-Net's encoder-decoder with skip connections can be reused for a different prediction target—noise rather than a segmentation mask.</p>
</section>
<section id="sampling"><h2>DDIM, guidance and latent diffusion</h2>
<ul><li><strong>DDIM:</strong> a sampling approach that can generate with fewer denoising steps than the basic stochastic DDPM procedure under suitable settings; fewer steps can trade off speed and quality.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> steers generation toward a condition, such as a text prompt. Classifier-free guidance combines conditional and unconditional predictions to strengthen conditioning, often at a diversity/quality trade-off.</li><li><strong>Latent diffusion:</strong> performs the diffusion process in a compressed latent representation produced by an encoder/decoder, reducing the spatial computation compared with operating directly on full-resolution pixels.</li></ul><p>More guidance is not always better: overly strong guidance can reduce diversity or introduce artifacts.</p>
</section>
<section id="evaluation"><h2>Evaluation and common misconceptions</h2>
<p>Generative quality includes realism, diversity, prompt/condition alignment and coverage of the data distribution. No single metric captures all these properties. FID compares feature distributions of generated and real samples; it is useful for controlled comparisons but depends on the feature extractor and sample setup.</p><ul><li>Discriminative models estimate labels/conditional outcomes; generative models learn to sample from data distributions.</li><li>The forward noising process is typically predefined; the neural network learns the reverse denoising behaviour.</li><li>Training often predicts noise, not necessarily the clean sample directly.</li><li>Generation requires multiple reverse steps in the standard diffusion setup.</li></ul>
</section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Week 11 questions and solutions</h2>
<p>Original practice based on the supplied diffusion-model lecture slides.</p><details class="question"><summary>Q1. What is the goal of the forward diffusion process?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Gradually corrupt clean data by adding noise.</p><p>The forward process defines noisy samples at increasing time steps; it is generally not the learned generation direction.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q2. What target does the common diffusion training objective ask the model to predict?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The noise added to the clean sample.</p><p>The loss compares predicted noise with the sampled Gaussian noise, often using MSE.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q3. What is the starting point for standard diffusion generation?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> A sample of random noise.</p><p>The learned reverse process iteratively denoises this sample into a structured output.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q4. Why can a U-Net be used as a diffusion denoiser?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Its encoder-decoder and skip connections combine broad context with fine spatial detail.</p><p>The same general architecture can predict noise instead of segmentation labels.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q5. What is the main speed benefit of DDIM-style sampling?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> It can use fewer denoising steps than a standard sampling schedule.</p><p>Reducing steps speeds generation, though the quality trade-off depends on configuration.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q6. What does latent diffusion do?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Runs diffusion in a compressed latent space instead of directly on full-resolution pixels.</p><p>A lower-dimensional representation can reduce memory and computation.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q7. What is a common trade-off of stronger classifier-free guidance?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Stronger condition alignment may come at the cost of diversity or artifacts.</p><p>Increasing the guidance scale can overemphasize the condition.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q8. True or false: the forward noising process is usually learned from scratch as part of the denoiser.</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> False</p><p>The forward corruption schedule is typically predefined; the neural network is trained to predict noise or reverse the corruption.</p></div></details>
</section>

